{"kind":"assert","text":"BO1 ruling: provisioning plan and wire shapes. Answer to d300b801 under request 41f77a01 and the boundary-objects note, section 9.\n\n1. Provisioning: a new explicit cohort, not a host seam. Genesis admits only a register with no creator (scope/src/creation-admission.ts:33); every other platform scope is created by a send from its creator, as the partition cohort shows (directory-partitions.ts: establish sends and create-partition). So a commons enters a room only through its directory, and the directory's sends are fixed per version. Adopt COMMONS_COHORT with register@23, directory@22, membership@9 and commons@1, keeping rules@10, destination@16 and inbox@1 from the unpublished cohort. directory@22 is directory@20 plus one establish send, create-commons, always, with result ref slot commons; register@23 maps to directory@22 in DIRECTORY_OF; membership@9 is membership@8's table plus one row, commons.write for all five roles (the same way @7 added application.install and @8 added directory.archive). Add the cohort to newest in scope/src/creation-versions.ts. The Workers' PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS stay as they are: newest comes from the code constants, so deployed genesis admits the new pins without a settings change, and existing pins keep their meanings. Rooms created before the cohort have no commons, as the note says.\n\n2. Catalog count: 80 to 84, not 81. The note's figure counted only commons@1; a kind cannot reach a room without a directory version to send it, a register version to map that directory, and a membership version to grant it. Update docs/kernel.md K1's count and list in the head. The landed note is not edited; the implementation note records the correction.\n\n3. Kernel gate. The head adds to packages/contract a scope kind and platform name (commons) and one field type (reference). Cite docs/kernel.md section 7 with this reason: a note's entry validity (its text bound, its media type, its reference shapes, its list bound) is what runtime and replay derive identically for every room, so it is a contract matter; it cannot be a package because packages install per room and the commons must exist in every new room before any package, including rooms that never install one; it cannot be a platform service because a service cannot decide what a recorded entry means. commons@1 adds no guards, effects, operations or K items.\n\n4. Reference shape. Add FieldType member { type: \"reference\"; fragment: number } whose value is a record of exactly one of document (a Digest of a retained file) or entry (a FactRef), plus an optional fragment string of at most that many bytes (512 for commons@1). The kernel checks shape only: digest form, FactRef form, fragment bound. It does not resolve the entry, check its kind or definition, read the referenced thing or interpret the fragment. FactRef stays closed; the fragment lives in the reference record, never inside FactRef. A reference creates no FactUse and binds nothing: no rule, guard, index, fold or view reads about, so a citation never becomes an authority-bearing typed fact. The fact FieldType remains the only typed, binding entry reference.\n\n5. Media type: no new field type. The note act has three fields: text, a detached text of at most 64 KiB; media, a text of at most 255 bytes holding an RFC 6838 type and subtype, required; about, a list of reference with max 64. The kernel checks media as a text bound only; the reader interprets it. The list bound is the contract's 64; entry size still bounds the whole.\n\n6. Package requirement. host.requires in application-codec/application-types.ts gains the member \"commons\" beside \"archive\". Placement of commons-missing: a guard has-commons on directory@22's act that admits a package into the application catalog, refusing commons-missing when the package requires commons and the directory's commons ref is absent (the shape of has-repository and no-repository in unpublished-directory.ts). Rooms with older directories run frozen code and cannot gain a refusal: there the host's install preparation (CLI and the install screen; BO3's slot list later) reports commons-missing before any act is signed, and the acceptance text says this is a host check, not a record refusal. Note that rules@10 refuses a non-archive install that carries any requires as unsupported install proof, so a commons-requiring package is refused in old rooms by that path too, and an archive install there records archive only. The native pins carrying the admission change are directory@22 alone among platform scopes; the codec change is in the platform package and bumps no package format version.\n\n7. Conditions: the whole cohort, the contract additions and the kernel-gate text in one head; native witnesses for create-commons at genesis, the note act with a document and an entry reference each carrying a fragment, a refused oversize fragment and a refused 65th reference, membership@9's five lists, and commons-missing on directory@22 with the commons ref absent; the W1 witness of 41f77a01 in a new hosted room on dap-demo after the planner deploys. One full gate; exact-head review by the checker citing docs/kernel.md section 7.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}